ple source, **BLOOD** can serve as “normal” tissue for genetic testing and is frequently used for germline mutation testing. It can also be used in certain molecular testing approaches alongside or sometimes instead of tumor tissue, including detection of tumor markers and tumor-related molecular features; in “liquid biopsy,” blood may be analyzed for cancer cells or circulating tumor DNA fragments (ctDNA).\n\nBiologically, **BLOOD** is also described as a pathway for cancer dissemination: cancer cells can travel through blood to lymph nodes and distant organs (hematogenous spread), leading to metastasis and contributing to the spread of some stage IV cancers. Blood is additionally referenced in symptom contexts (e.g., blood noted in urine or with rectal bleeding) and in physiology (e.g., menstrual blood passage). It is also relevant to infectious disease transmission, as hepatitis B and C can spread through contact with infected blood, and blood is the medium reached by intravenous chemotherapy and certain injected tracers used in imaging.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 550, "prompt_tokens": 1387, "total_tokens": 1937, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}